<article class="env-lemma" id="0749">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0749">Lemma <span data-tag="0749">11.4.1</span>.</a> Let $A$, $A'$ be $k$-algebras. Let $B \subset A$, $B' \subset A'$ be subalgebras with centralizers $C$, $C'$. Then the centralizer of $B \otimes _ k B'$ in $A \otimes _ k A'$ is $C \otimes _ k C'$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Denote $C'' \subset A \otimes _ k A'$ the centralizer of $B \otimes _ k B'$. It is clear that $C \otimes _ k C' \subset C''$. Conversely, every element of $C''$ commutes with $B \otimes 1$ hence is contained in $C \otimes _ k A'$. Similarly $C'' \subset A \otimes _ k C'$. Thus $C'' \subset C \otimes _ k A' \cap A \otimes _ k C' = C \otimes _ k C'$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>